= Research: Toward a predictive science of deep learning

My goal is to uncover the principles governing deep learning and use them to explain mechanisms, make quantitative predictions, and inform algorithm design. I pursue this goal through three complementary perspectives:

- *Physics.* Finding the right level of description: deciding which details matter at a given scale and building toy models to isolate the underlying mechanisms.

- *Mathematics.* Establishing rigorous results under explicit conditions and clarifying the scope of a theory.

- *Engineering.* Stress-testing theory in real-world systems and refining it through discrepancies between predictions and observations.

Inspired by physics, I view *scale as an organizing principle for deep learning*. I do not expect a single theory to capture learning across all scales; different scales require different [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Effective_theory effective theories].

~~~
{}{raw}
<div class="research-figure">
<img src="./doc/physics-dl.svg" alt="Different scales, different effective theories in physics and deep learning" style="display: block; width: 100%; max-width: 1000px; height: auto; margin: 0 auto;" />
</div>
~~~

This perspective suggests two complementary routes toward understanding deep learning:

- *Bottom-up.* Starting from simple models and microscopic mechanisms and working toward more complex learning systems.
- *Top-down.* Starting from macroscopic laws and using them to guide the study of underlying mechanisms and intermediate-scale dynamics.

Much of my earlier work followed the bottom-up route. Yet the connection to real-world training often proved fragile. Without guidance from  macroscopic regularities, microscopic modeling and analysis leave too much freedom: different choices of assumptions, mechanisms, and regimes can yield very different conclusions, with little guidance on which matter at the macroscopic level. I am now exploring the top-down route, using such regularities to guide the construction and analysis of microscopic toy models, so that the two routes can inform one another.

== Current focus:  LLM pretraining

~~~
{}{raw}
<div class="research-focus-grid">
<div class="research-focus-text">
~~~

[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Large_language_model Large language model (LLM)] pretraining optimizes a next-token prediction objective at an enormous scale:

\(
  \min_{\theta} \mathcal{R}(\theta),\qquad
  \mathcal{R}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}\sim\mathcal{D}}
  \left[-\sum_{k=2}^{L}\log P_{\theta}(x_k\mid x_1,\dots,x_{k-1})\right].
\)

Here, $P_\theta$ is a Transformer language model, and $\mathcal{D}$ is an internet-scale text corpus. Despite its complex training dynamics, pretraining exhibits simple macroscopic regularities, such as [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Neural_scaling_law scaling laws]. My research seeks to explain these regularities and use them to guide training through three connected levels:


- *Macroscopic laws.* Identify collective variables that capture pretraining and quantitative laws relating them across scales.

- *Microscopic mechanisms.* Understand how these laws emerge from high-dimensional learning dynamics.

- *Training principles.*  Guide optimizer and architecture design and hyperparameter selection as training scales up.


~~~
{}{raw}
</div>
<div class="research-figure research-focus-figure">
<img src="./doc/llm-pretraining-overview.svg" alt="Macroscopic laws guide microscopic models, which inform practical training; experiments test and refine the laws." />
</div>
</div>
~~~

For an overview, see my [./doc/LLM-pretraining-theory.pdf talk slides]. Selected work includes:

- *Functional scaling law (FSL).* Modeling loss dynamics as a functional of hyperparameter schedules:
[https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.19189 NeurIPS 2025], [https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.14208 ICLR 2026], [https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.06797 COLT 2026].

- *ELR collapse.* Pretraining  dynamics are governed primarily by  effective learning rate (ELR), rather than the nominal LR:
[https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.24814 arXiv 2026].

- *Optimizer mechanism and designing.* Understanding how optimizer design shapes training dynamics:
[https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.02728 arXiv 2026], [https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.19002 ICML 2025].

== Earlier work

My earlier work developed mathematical perspectives on neural-network function spaces and SGD dynamics. These studies helped shape my current approach to understanding large-scale learning.

- *Dynamical stability of SGD.* Why does SGD favor some solutions over others that fit the data equally well? We studied this question through dynamical stability and the geometry of SGD noise, shedding light on implicit regularization in overparameterized learning.
-- [doc/dynamical-stability.pdf Talk slides]
-- Selected papers: [https://papers.nips.cc/paper/8049-how-sgd-selects-the-global-minima-in-over-parameterized-learning-a-dynamical-stability-perspective NeurIPS 2018], [https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.02628 NeurIPS 2022], [https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.17490 ICML 2023], and [https://openreview.net/forum?id=F6wmIilZXG TMLR 2026].

- *Function-space theory of neural networks.* When and why can neural networks efficiently approximate and learn functions in high dimensions, and how do these capabilities differ from those of kernel and random-feature methods? We studied these questions through the function spaces associated with neural networks, connecting approximation properties, architectural inductive biases, and generalization. 
-- [doc/function-space.pdf Talk slides]
-- Selected papers: [https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.08039 Constructive Approximation 2021], [https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.04964 JMLR 2022], [https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.08404 NeurIPS 2023], and [https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.05642 AoS 2025].


